// boxcxx — <csignal>
//
// [support.signal]. What this header does NOT do is the part worth reading
// first: **nothing in BoxOS ever raises a signal at your process.** There is
// no asynchronous delivery here, by decision, not by omission — BoxOS already
// has an event vocabulary, and it is Touch: a tag-multicast event with a
// named subscriber, delivered where the subscriber asked for it. Mapping the
// CPU's faults onto handler callbacks would have been building a second,
// older, worse one alongside it.
//
// That does not make the header a stub. The standard never requires an
// implementation to raise a signal on its own: [support.signal] specifies
// `signal` (install), `raise` (deliver, right here, on this strand) and what
// the two default dispositions mean. All of that is real below, and
// std::abort is specified in terms of it — abort() is `raise(SIGABRT)`
// followed by termination whether the handler returns or ignores it, which is
// exactly what happens. Ported code that installs a SIGABRT handler to print
// a last word before dying gets its last word.
//
// The signal NUMBERS follow the same de-facto convention <__bits/errc> uses
// for its values, for the same reason: a number a ported program may already
// have written down (`if (sig == 6)`) should mean what it means everywhere
// else. The exit status of a default-disposition death is 128 + signal, which
// is the convention boxcxx's own Panic has been using since Ф1 (it exits 134,
// and 134 is 128 + SIGABRT).
#ifndef BOXCXX_CSIGNAL
#define BOXCXX_CSIGNAL

// [csignal.syn] gives the handler type C language linkage. The alias is
// declared inside a linkage specification, which is what puts the *function
// type* — not just some function — into extern "C", so a handler written in C
// and a handler written in C++ are the same type here.
extern "C" {
using __boxcxx_signal_handler = void(int);
}

namespace std {

// [support.signal]/2: an integer type an object of which can be accessed as an
// atomic entity even in the presence of asynchronous signals. On x86-64 a
// naturally aligned int is exactly that.
using sig_atomic_t = int;

// Installs `func` as the disposition of `sig` and returns the previous one, or
// SIG_ERR if `sig` is not one of the six below (or `func` is SIG_ERR itself).
// The disposition is NOT reset to SIG_DFL when a handler runs — C leaves that
// choice to the implementation, and a handler that has to reinstall itself
// after every delivery is a race waiting for a second strand.
__boxcxx_signal_handler *signal(int sig, __boxcxx_signal_handler *func) noexcept;

// Delivers `sig` to the calling strand NOW, on this stack, and returns zero on
// success. SIG_IGN returns immediately; SIG_DFL ends the process with status
// 128 + sig; anything else is called with `sig` and raise returns when it does.
int raise(int sig) noexcept;

} // namespace std

// As with <cctype>, the names are also visible unqualified: ported code writes
// signal(SIGABRT, h), and [headers]/5 leaves that free.
using std::raise;
using std::sig_atomic_t;
using std::signal;

// The three dispositions. Distinct values, none of which can be the address of
// a declarable function: 0 is the null pointer and 1 and (uintptr_t)-1 are not
// aligned to any function's entry.
#define SIG_DFL (static_cast<__boxcxx_signal_handler *>(nullptr))
#define SIG_IGN (reinterpret_cast<__boxcxx_signal_handler *>(1))
#define SIG_ERR (reinterpret_cast<__boxcxx_signal_handler *>(-1))

// The six [support.signal] requires, and no more: a macro named SIGKILL or
// SIGPIPE would name a concept BoxOS does not have.
#define SIGINT   2
#define SIGILL   4
#define SIGABRT  6
#define SIGFPE   8
#define SIGSEGV 11
#define SIGTERM 15

#endif // BOXCXX_CSIGNAL
